{
 "ep": 24,
 "total": 140,
 "title": "Connection Pooling with PgBouncer",
 "phase": "Installation & Administration",
 "lines": [
  {
   "say": "How connection pooling protects PostgreSQL.",
   "cap": "How *connection pooling* protects *PostgreSQL.*"
  },
  {
   "say": "This is episode twenty four of the PostgreSQL DBA series. Today: PgBouncer, its three pool modes, and the traps.",
   "cap": "Episode 24: *PgBouncer,* pool modes and *traps.*",
   "cue": "modes"
  },
  {
   "say": "Every PostgreSQL connection costs a whole server process.",
   "cap": "Every connection = *1 server process.*",
   "cue": "process"
  },
  {
   "say": "PgBouncer is a connection pooler. Apps connect to it, by default on port six four three two, as if it were PostgreSQL.",
   "cap": "Apps connect to *PgBouncer* on *6432,* like PostgreSQL.",
   "cue": "port"
  },
  {
   "say": "PgBouncer keeps a small set of real server connections, and reuses them for many clients.",
   "cap": "A few *server connections,* reused by *many clients.*",
   "cue": "reuses"
  },
  {
   "say": "max_client_conn caps the client connections. The default is one hundred.",
   "cap": "*max_client_conn:* default *100* clients.",
   "cue": "hundred"
  },
  {
   "say": "default_pool_size is the server connections per user and database pair. The default is twenty.",
   "cap": "*default_pool_size:* *20* per user and database.",
   "cue": "twenty"
  },
  {
   "say": "The key question: when does a server connection go back to the pool? That is pool_mode.",
   "cap": "*pool_mode:* when is a server *released?*",
   "cue": "pool_mode"
  },
  {
   "say": "Session mode is the default. The server goes back when the client disconnects.",
   "cap": "*session* (default): when the client *disconnects.*",
   "cue": "disconnects"
  },
  {
   "say": "Transaction mode: the server goes back as soon as the transaction finishes.",
   "cap": "*transaction:* when the *transaction finishes.*",
   "cue": "finishes"
  },
  {
   "say": "Statement mode: after every query, and transactions with several statements are not allowed.",
   "cap": "*statement:* after each query. *No multi statement* transactions.",
   "cue": "allowed"
  },
  {
   "say": "Let's test transaction mode. In a lab, default_pool_size is two, and ten clients each run a six second query.",
   "cap": "Lab: *pool size 2,* *10 clients* at once.",
   "cue": "ten"
  },
  {
   "say": "On the admin console, SHOW POOLS, output trimmed, says two clients active, eight waiting, and two server connections.",
   "cap": "*2 active,* *8 waiting,* *2 server* connections.",
   "cue": "eight"
  },
  {
   "say": "pg_stat_activity agrees: only two app_user backends, for ten clients.",
   "cap": "PostgreSQL sees only *2 backends.*",
   "cue": "two"
  },
  {
   "say": "Now the catch. In transaction mode, your next transaction may run on another server connection, and server_reset_query is not used.",
   "cap": "Next transaction, *maybe another server.* No reset query.",
   "cue": "another"
  },
  {
   "say": "So session state can leak. One client runs SET work_mem to sixty four megabytes.",
   "cap": "Client A: *SET work_mem = 64MB.*",
   "cue": "SET"
  },
  {
   "say": "A brand new client connects, and SHOW work_mem says sixty four megabytes, not the default of four.",
   "cap": "Client B sees *64MB,* not *4MB.*",
   "cue": "sixty"
  },
  {
   "say": "The PgBouncer docs say: no session based features in transaction mode.",
   "cap": "No *session features* in transaction mode.",
   "cue": "session"
  },
  {
   "say": "That means session settings, prepared statements, LISTEN, advisory locks and temp tables.",
   "cap": "Settings, PREPARE, LISTEN, *locks,* *temp tables.*",
   "cue": "LISTEN"
  },
  {
   "say": "Need a setting for one transaction? SET LOCAL lasts until commit or rollback. Our next client saw four megabytes.",
   "cap": "Use *SET LOCAL:* gone after *COMMIT.*",
   "cue": "LOCAL"
  },
  {
   "say": "Prepared statements from drivers need max_prepared_statements above zero.",
   "cap": "*max_prepared_statements* above *0* for drivers.",
   "cue": "zero"
  },
  {
   "say": "In version one point two two, it ships at zero, which disables that support.",
   "cap": "PgBouncer 1.22 ships it at *0:* *disabled.*",
   "cue": "zero"
  },
  {
   "say": "Common mistake: switching an app to transaction mode without checking its session features.",
   "cap": "Mistake: switch modes *without checking the app.*",
   "cue": "mistake"
  },
  {
   "say": "Recap: PgBouncer lends a few server connections to many clients. Session mode is the default.",
   "cap": "Recap: *few servers,* *many clients.*",
   "cue": "default"
  },
  {
   "say": "Transaction mode shares the most, but session state does not follow you. Use SET LOCAL, and watch waiting clients.",
   "cap": "*SET LOCAL,* and watch *cl_waiting.*",
   "cue": "waiting"
  },
  {
   "say": "Next up: tablespaces, when and why to use them. Follow so you don't miss it, and send this to a friend who's learning PostgreSQL.",
   "cap": "Next up: *tablespaces.* *Follow* and share."
  }
 ],
